  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Leaving rural Rogers after high school with borrowed money, Deason found clerical work in Tulsa’s petroleum sector. He shifted into computing operations, eventually becoming chief executive of Dallas data processor MTech at 29. When EDS acquired that business, he immediately assembled a new venture around recurring back-office contracts for corporate and government clients.\n\nAffiliated Computer Services evolved from bank data processing into one of the largest outsourced-services groups in the world. As chairman and strategist, Deason pursued acquisitions, expanded public-sector programmes and built a Fortune 500 company employing tens of thousands. Its 2010 acquisition created his defining financial exit. He subsequently directed Deason Capital Services and funded education, criminal-justice reform, medical research and Christian organisations through his foundation.",
    "html": "<p>Leaving rural Rogers after high school with borrowed money, Deason found clerical work in Tulsa’s petroleum sector. He shifted into computing operations, eventually becoming chief executive of Dallas data processor MTech at 29. When EDS acquired that business, he immediately assembled a new venture around recurring back-office contracts for corporate and government clients.</p>\r\n<p>Affiliated Computer Services evolved from bank data processing into one of the largest outsourced-services groups in the world. As chairman and strategist, Deason pursued acquisitions, expanded public-sector programmes and built a Fortune 500 company employing tens of thousands. Its 2010 acquisition created his defining financial exit. He subsequently directed Deason Capital Services and funded education, criminal-justice reform, medical research and Christian organisations through his foundation.</p>\r\n",
